course: dictionary
lesson: 2380
title: A cuppa and a chat
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: Some words belong to the everyday speech of one part of the world. Cuppa is one of them. In Britain, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, people often use it to mean a cup of tea.
explain: «Fancy a cuppa?» is a friendly way to ask if someone would like a drink. The context helps: the speaker is offering something to drink, and fancy means would you like.
explain: Putting the kettle on is a way of welcoming and comforting people. Words like this can show a feeling as well as a meaning, so read the whole moment, not just the one word. «Waiting, missing someone or wanting a cuppa makes time feel longer than the clock says.»
explain: Safety first: kettles and hot tea can burn, so always ask an adult before you make a cuppa.
fact: Cuppa is an informal noun. It means a cup of tea.
fact: Less often, cuppa can mean a cup of any hot drink, such as coffee.
fact: «Put the kettle on for a cuppa» means make a cup of tea.
fact: In stories, a cuppa often shows a cosy, friendly or calm moment, or comfort after bad news.
fact: Cuppa is used most in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
exercise: Mum asks, «Fancy a cuppa?» What is she offering? | A bath | A walk in the park | *A cup of tea | A bag of sweets
exercise: Grandma says, «Put the kettle on, love, you've had a rough day.» What does putting the kettle on show? | *A way of welcoming and comforting someone | A way of leaving the house | A rule about bedtime | A sign that the power has gone off
exercise: Find and copy one word from «Waiting, missing someone or wanting a cuppa makes time feel longer than the clock says.» that means a cup of tea. | feel | time | clock | *cuppa
test: «A nice cuppa and a biscuit» most likely means | A new pair of shoes | A long walk to school | A cold swim and a sandwich | *A cup of tea and a biscuit
test: Which is a safety rule when making a cuppa? | Use the kettle alone at night | Pour boiling water on your hands to warm them | *Ask an adult, because kettles and hot tea can burn | Carry the full kettle across the room without looking
exam: In the UK, what does cuppa most often mean? | A type of biscuit | *A cup of tea | A small plate | A cup of ice cream
